solution:
The midpoint of the segment is $\left(\dfrac{-2+8}{2},\dfrac{4+4}{2}\right)=(3,4)$. Both endpoints have $y=4$, so the segment is horizontal, and the line perpendicular to it is vertical. The vertical line through $(3,4)$ is $x=3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
xaxis(Label("$x$",position=EndPoint,align=3E),-4,10,Arrow(6));
yaxis(Label("$y$",position=EndPoint,align=3N),-1,7,Arrow(6));
pair P=(-2,4),Q=(8,4),M=(3,4);
draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,-1)--(3,7),blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,4)--(3.4,4)--(3.4,4.4)--(3,4.4));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(M);
label("$(-2,4)$",P,N);label("$(8,4)$",Q,N);label("$M(3,4)$",M+(0.3,-0.1),SE);
label("$x=3$",(3,6.3),E,blue);
draw((0.5,3.85)--(0.5,4.15));draw((5.5,3.85)--(5.5,4.15));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The answer is \textbf{(A)} $x=3$. (Every point $(3,y)$ is at distance $\sqrt{25+(y-4)^2}$ from both endpoints, as a perpendicular bisector must be.)

A line through $(1,1)$ with slope $m$ is $y=1+m(x-1)$; it meets the $y$-axis at $y=1-m$. The condition is $2<1-m<3$, that is, $\boxed{-2<m<-1}$. The boundary slopes are those of the lines through $(1,1)$ and $(0,2)$ (slope $-1$) and through $(1,1)$ and $(0,3)$ (slope $-2$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
fill((1,1)--(0,2)--(0,3)--cycle,paleblue);
xaxis(Label("$x$",position=EndPoint,align=3E),-0.6,2.4,Arrow(6));
yaxis(Label("$y$",position=EndPoint,align=3N),-0.4,3.8,Arrow(6));
pair P=(1,1),A=(0,2),B=(0,3);
draw((1.6,-0.2)--(0,3),dashed);draw((1.8,0.2)--(0,2),dashed);
draw((1.7,-0.05)--(0,2.5),red+linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);dot(A);dot(B);
label("$(1,1)$",P,E);label("$(0,2)$",A,W);label("$(0,3)$",B,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the figure the dashed lines are the two boundary lines (slopes $-1$ and $-2$, not included), and the red line, with slope $-\dfrac{3}{2}$, is one admissible line.

Since the ball touches all six faces, it is inscribed in the cube: if the ball has radius $r$, the cube has edge $s=2r$. The figure shows the cross-section through the centre $O$ parallel to a face.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4;
draw(box((0,0),(s,s)),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((s/2,s/2),s/2),blue+linewidth(0.8));
pair O=(s/2,s/2);dot(O);
draw(O--(s,s/2),dashed);
label("$r$",(3*s/4,s/2),N);
draw((0,-0.35)--(s,-0.35),Arrows(5));label("$s=2r$",(s/2,-0.35),S);
label("$O$",O,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The ball's volume is $\dfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3=\dfrac{\pi}{6}s^3=18\text{ dL}=1800$ mL, so the cube holds $s^3=\dfrac{6\cdot1800}{\pi}=\dfrac{10800}{\pi}$ mL. When the ball goes in, $1800$ mL of water overflows; when it is taken out, the water left is
\[
\dfrac{10800}{\pi}-1800\approx\boxed{1638\text{ mL}}
\]
(exactly $1800\left(\dfrac{6}{\pi}-1\right)\approx1637.7$ mL).

question:
A polyhedron has an inscribed sphere of radius $r$, that is, a sphere tangent to every face. Let $V$ be the volume of the polyhedron and $S$ its total surface area.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $V=\dfrac{1}{3}rS$ (the analogue of the plane formula area $=$ inradius $\times$ semiperimeter).
\item[(b)] Use (a) to find the radius of the sphere inscribed in a regular tetrahedron of edge $6$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $O$ be the centre of the inscribed sphere. Join $O$ to every vertex; this cuts the polyhedron into pyramids, one for each face, with apex $O$ and that face as base. Since the sphere is tangent to the face, the perpendicular from $O$ to the plane of the face has length $r$, so every pyramid has height $r$. In the figure (a tetrahedron) the pyramid $OABC$ is drawn, with its height $r$ in red.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
triple[] T={(0,0,0),(6,0,0),(3,3*sqrt(3),0),(3,sqrt(3),2*sqrt(6))};
pair P(triple t){return (t.x+0.45*t.y,t.z+t.y);}
pair A=P(T[0]),B=P(T[1]),C=P(T[2]),D=P(T[3]);
pair G=P((3,sqrt(3),0)),O=P((3,sqrt(3),sqrt(6)/2));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(B--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed);
draw(O--A,blue+dashed);draw(O--B,blue+dashed);draw(O--C,blue+dashed);
draw(O--G,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);dot(G);
label("$O$",O,E);label("$r$",(O+G)/2,W,red);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

If the faces have areas $S_1,\dots,S_k$, then
\[
V=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\dfrac{1}{3}S_ir=\dfrac{1}{3}r\left(S_1+\cdots+S_k\right)=\dfrac{1}{3}rS .\qquad\blacksquare
\]

\textbf{(b)} A regular tetrahedron of edge $a=6$ has four equilateral faces, so $S=4\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}a^2=36\sqrt3$. Its height is $a\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{3}}=2\sqrt6$ (the centroid of a face is at distance $\dfrac{a}{\sqrt3}=2\sqrt3$ from each vertex, and $36-12=24$), so $V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot9\sqrt3\cdot2\sqrt6=18\sqrt2$. Then
\[
r=\dfrac{3V}{S}=\dfrac{54\sqrt2}{36\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}} .
\]

The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so with centre $O$ the half-diagonals are $1$ and $2$, and each side is $\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\sqrt5$. The area is $\dfrac{2\cdot4}{2}=4$. The inscribed circle has centre $O$ and touches each side, so its radius $r$ is the height from $O$ in each of the four right triangles $OAB,OBC,OCD,ODA$ with hypotenuse $\sqrt5$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-2,0),B=(0,-1),C=(2,0),D=(0,1),O=(0,0);
real r=2/sqrt(5);
pair T=O+r*dir(degrees(D-C)+90+180);
pair u=unit(D-C); pair T2=C+dot(O-C,u)*u;
fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,paleblue);
filldraw(circle(O,r),white,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed);draw(B--D,dashed);
draw(O--T2,red+linewidth(0.8));
pair v=unit(O-T2);
draw(T2+0.12*u--T2+0.12*u+0.12*v--T2+0.12*v);
dot(O);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$r$",(O+T2)/2,dir(-27),red);
label("$2$",(-1,0),S);label("$1$",(0,-0.5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence
\[
4=4\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\sqrt5\cdot r\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt5},\qquad r^2=\dfrac{4}{5} .
\]
The area outside the circle is
\[
4-\dfrac{4\pi}{5}\approx4-2.513=1.487\approx\boxed{1.5}\text{ square feet}.
\]
The student's error is the base of the triangles: each right triangle $OAB$ has hypotenuse $AB=\sqrt5$, not $2\sqrt5$ (the half-diagonals are $1$ and $2$, not $2$ and $4$).
